==============================
FreeDCE RPC Toolkit for Linux
==============================

This package provides support for developing DCE RPC applications on the
Linux platform, which interoperate with other DCE RPC and Microsoft RPC
implementations.  The source code and binaries are freely redistributable
provided you maintain the copyright notices of the source components.

FreeDCE uses the POSIX threads of the GNU C library (NPTL).  The OSF code
expects DCE threads (the pthreads draft 4 interface) with DCE exceptions
and DCE cancel semantics; the dcethreads library (directory dcethreads)
emulates them on top of POSIX threads, so that multithreaded RPC clients
and servers coexist with other threaded and non-threaded libraries.  It
is tested on x86_64 (64-bit).

The package could be ported to other platforms as well, provided that you
can implement the semantics of DCE exceptions and DCE threads on the
target platform's thread layer.

Inventory of Goods:
-------------------
* OSF DCE 1.1 IDL compiler (dceidl, also installed as idl)
* DCE RPC runtime and development libraries/headers
* `uuid' tool for generating and manipulating DCE UUIDs
* rpcd - the host endpoint mapper for DCE RPC applications
* perf test suite (freedce/perf) and a load test (freedce/tests/loadtest)
* demonstration clients and servers (freedce/demos)
* experimental DCOM support (configure --enable-dcom; off by default, not
  maintained)

Prerequisites:
-------------

A C compiler with C23 support (the IDL compiler uses it; gcc 13 or
later), a C++ compiler (for the uuid tool), make, autoconf, automake,
libtool, flex and bison.  Tested on x86_64 with:

 Ubuntu 26.04:  gcc 15.2, autoconf 2.72, automake 1.18, libtool 2.5.4
 AlmaLinux 10:  gcc 14.3, autoconf 2.71, automake 1.16.5, libtool 2.4.7
 Debian 13:     gcc 14.2, autoconf 2.72, automake 1.17, libtool 2.5.4
 Arch Linux:    gcc 16.2, autoconf 2.73, automake 1.19, libtool 2.6.2

and flex 2.6.4, bison 3.8.2 on all of them.  The packages of 2.0.0 were
also built and tested on Ubuntu 24.04 (gcc 13.3), Fedora 44 (gcc 16.2),
openSUSE Tumbleweed (gcc 16.2) and Leap 16.0 (gcc 15.1), and on
AlmaLinux 9 with gcc-toolset-14 (its gcc 11 cannot compile the C23 IDL
compiler).  The packages:

 Debian, Ubuntu:
  # apt install build-essential autoconf automake libtool flex bison
 RHEL 10 and compatible (AlmaLinux 10):
  # dnf install gcc gcc-c++ make autoconf automake libtool flex bison \
      diffutils file
 RHEL 9 and compatible: as RHEL 10, plus gcc-toolset-14-gcc and
  gcc-toolset-14-gcc-c++; build after ". /opt/rh/gcc-toolset-14/enable"
 Fedora: as RHEL 10
 openSUSE:
  # zypper install gcc gcc-c++ make autoconf automake libtool flex bison
 Arch Linux:
  # pacman -S --needed base-devel

How to Build:
-------------

FreeDCE needs the dcethreads library (directory dcethreads of the
repository); build and install it first, with the same prefix.  In a git
checkout, buildconf creates the configure scripts.  From the top
directory of the repository:

 % cd dcethreads
 % ./buildconf
 % ./configure --prefix=/opt/dce
 % make
 % make install
 % cd ../freedce
 % ./buildconf
 % ./configure --prefix=/opt/dce
 % make
 % make install

/opt/dce is the default prefix; pass the same --prefix to both configure
calls to change it.  "make install" needs write access to the prefix.
Building out of tree (configure called from an empty directory) works as
well.  With another autoconf version, buildconf rewrites the comments of
the tracked templates dcethreads_conf.h.in and dce_config.h.in.

Tests: "make check" in dcethreads, in freedce/perf (one test needs a
running rpcd) and in freedce/tests/loadtest (a short load test).

The installed programs find the libraries through their run path; other
programs need LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/dce/lib or an entry in
/etc/ld.so.conf.d.  The endpoint mapper rpcd listens on port 135 and has
to run as root:

 # /opt/dce/bin/rpcd

(-f keeps it in the foreground.)  rpcd keeps its endpoint database
(rpcdep.dat) in /tmp/; configure --with-rpcd-dbdir=DIR changes that.  The
install does not start rpcd at boot; freedce/rpcd contains a systemd unit
(freedce-rpcd.service, installed by the packages) and the old SysV init
scripts rc.dcerpcd and rc.dce-clean.

Packages: packaging/ builds .deb packages (Debian, Ubuntu), .rpm packages
(Fedora, RHEL/AlmaLinux/Rocky 9 and 10, openSUSE) and an Arch Linux
package from a source tarball; see packaging/README.  They install below
/opt/dce as well, with the systemd unit freedce-rpcd.service (not enabled)
and the rpcd database in /var/opt/freedce.  Check downloaded packages with
"sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS" before installing them.

How to build your own programs that use DCE RPC
-----------------------------------------------

CFLAGS+=-D_REENTRANT -D_GNU_SOURCE -I/opt/dce/include
LDFLAGS+=-L/opt/dce/lib -Wl,-rpath,/opt/dce/lib
LIBS+=-ldcerpc -ldcethreads -lpthread

Generate the stubs with the IDL compiler, /opt/dce/bin/dceidl.
The UUID functions (uuid_create etc.) are part of libdcerpc; do not link
-luuid (that is the unrelated util-linux library). A program that uses the
util-linux libuuid itself must call it from a separate shared library: both
libraries define uuid_compare, with different arguments.

Error texts: dce_error_inq_text() knows a text for every status code of
rpcsts.idl and ncastat.idl, followed by the symbolic name, e.g.
"connection closed (rpc_s_connection_closed) (dce / rpc)".  The texts are
built into libdcerpc (freedce/ncklib/rpc/dce_error_texts.txt and
mkerrtab.awk); a message catalog (dcerpc.cat) found through NLSPATH takes
precedence.

Runtime settings (ncacn_ip_tcp)
-------------------------------

RPC_CN_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT: the idle time in seconds after which a server
asks a client to close its connection (default 120, 0 = never).  A
connection that the client neither closes nor uses in the 30 seconds after
that is closed by the server.  The last connection of a client that holds
context handles is kept.  When accept runs out of descriptors, the server
reclaims connections idle for 5 seconds at once (shutdown request, closed
after 5 seconds).

Bind timeout: a client gives up waiting for the answer to its bind after
6 times the com timeout of the binding (rpc_mgmt_set_com_timeout; 30
seconds by default, at least 5 seconds, no limit with
rpc_c_binding_infinite_timeout) with rpc_s_connect_timed_out.

Vanished peers: servers and clients set SO_KEEPALIVE on their connections,
with per-connection times, so that a peer host that goes away (power loss,
network gone, new address) is detected after about 2 minutes; the call or
the contexts of the client then end as for a closed connection.
RPC_CN_KEEPALIVE="idle,intvl,count": seconds of idle time before the first
probe, seconds between probes, and probes (default 60,10,6).
RPC_CN_USER_TIMEOUT: seconds after which data the peer has not acknowledged
aborts the connection (default idle + intvl x count, i.e. 120; Linux also
ends the keepalive probing by this time).  0 for either leaves the kernel
settings (net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time/intvl/probes, about 2 hours, and
net.ipv4.tcp_retries2, about 15 minutes) in effect.  A live peer answers
the probes, so long-running calls are not affected.

